🚀 Mini Project:
Deploy a Simple App + Health Check using curl + Docker

👉 Goal:

Run an app in Docker

Expose an API

Use curl for health check

Simulate DevOps monitoring

🧱 Step 1: Create a Simple App

We’ll use a Node.js app (very basic).

👉 Create file: app.js

const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    if (req.url === '/health') {
        res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'application/json'});
        res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'UP' }));
    } else {
        res.writeHead(200);
        res.end('Hello DevOps 🚀');
    }
});

server.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
📦 Step 2: Create Dockerfile

👉 Create Dockerfile

FROM node:18

WORKDIR /app

COPY app.js .

CMD ["node", "app.js"]
🐳 Step 3: Build & Run Container
docker build -t devops-app .
docker run -d -p 3000:3000 devops-app
🌐 Step 4: Test App Using curl

👉 Normal request:

curl http://localhost:3000

👉 Health check:

curl http://localhost:3000/health

✔ Output:

{"status":"UP"}
🔥 Step 5: DevOps Health Check Script

👉 Create health-check.sh

#!/bin/bash

URL="http://localhost:3000/health"

response=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" $URL)

if [ "$response" -eq 200 ]; then
  echo "✅ Application is UP"
else
  echo "❌ Application is DOWN"
  exit 1
fi

👉 Run:

chmod +x health-check.sh
./health-check.sh
🔁 Step 6: Continuous Monitoring (Like DevOps)
while true; do ./health-check.sh; sleep 5; done

✔ Checks app every 5 seconds
✔ Simulates monitoring system

💥 Step 7: Simulate Failure

👉 Stop container:

docker stop <container_id>

👉 Now script output:

❌ Application is DOWN

✔ This is exactly how alerts work in real companies

